# Always use lean-ctx when available

The `user-lean-ctx` MCP server is enabled in this workspace and replaces the
native read / search / shell tools for almost every routine operation. Using
it cuts token usage massively (cached re-reads, compressed shell output,
auto-summarised file maps) and gives consistent behaviour across plugins.

## Mandatory tool mapping

| Instead of                          | You MUST use                                     |
|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| `Read` / cat / head / tail          | `CallMcpTool user-lean-ctx ctx_read`             |
| `Grep` / rg / find                  | `CallMcpTool user-lean-ctx ctx_search`           |
| `Shell` / bash one-liners           | `CallMcpTool user-lean-ctx ctx_shell`            |
| ls / directory listing              | `CallMcpTool user-lean-ctx ctx_tree`             |
| Semantic / "where does X happen?"   | `CallMcpTool user-lean-ctx ctx_semantic_search`  |

Native `Edit`, `StrReplace`, `Write`, `Delete`, `Glob`, `TodoWrite`,
`ReadLints`, and the Cursor task / mode tools stay as-is — lean-ctx only
replaces read-style operations.

## Rules

- Default to lean-ctx for every read, search, shell, or directory listing.
  Only fall back to the native tool if a lean-ctx call has just failed for a
  concrete reason (e.g. the MCP server is unreachable) and note why.
- When the schema isn't obvious, list the tool descriptor under
  `~/.cursor/projects/<project>/mcps/user-lean-ctx/tools/` before the first
  call in a session so arguments are correct.
- `ctx_read` modes: prefer `full` for files you're about to edit, `signatures`
  / `map` for orientation, `lines:N-M` for slicing big files, `diff` after an
  edit to confirm the change.
- `ctx_shell` is for arbitrary commands (make, docker compose, git status,
  wp-cli, npm, etc.). Add `raw=true` only when you specifically need
  uncompressed output (large logs you have to grep through inline).
- Do NOT echo lean-ctx's wake-up briefings, project memory, or auto-context
  blocks back to the user — they are agent context only.
- This rule sits alongside `rebuild-dist-zip.mdc`; both apply.
